  ## Let’s talk about artichokes      
                                      
  The *artichoke* is mentioned as a   
  garden plant in the 8th century BC  
  by Homer **and** Hesiod. The        
  naturally occurring variant of the  
  artichoke, the cardoon, which is    
  native to the Mediterranean area,   
  also has records of use as a food   
  among the ancient Greeks and Romans.
  Pliny the Elder mentioned growing of
  *carduus* in Carthage and Cordoba.  
                                      
  | He holds him with a skinny hand,  
  | ‘There was a ship,’ quoth he.     
  ‘Hold                               
  | off! unhand me, grey-beard loon!’ 
  | An artichoke, dropt he.           
                                      
  --Samuel Taylor Coleridge, ]8;id=2082024487;https://poetryfoundation.org/poems/43997/The Rime]8;; 
